# misc
.DS_Store
.env.local
.env.development
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local
.env.production

npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# No .env file is tracked. .env.development was tracked for a while so a clean
# clone would come up working, but a working config is by definition a file full
# of real credentials — the copy in history held a JWT signing secret, a VAPID
# private key and a commented-out production database password. A new developer
# runs `cp .env.example .env.development` instead; .env.example documents every
# variable the code reads and is the file to keep in sync.
# mysqldump output. Real rows mean real people — 555 staff email addresses and
# the financial history were readable to anyone who cloned this. Developers get
# a dump out of band and restore it locally; it never enters git again.
/schema/dump/
